      "id": 1,
      "text": "Swansea incorporated on December 20, 1886 after residents resisted annexation by Belleville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        14,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swansea%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Village Hall was completed shortly after incorporation, costing $619.50 and serving as the town's first civic center.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swansea%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1895 an ordinance changed the village name from “New Swansea” to “Swansea” to match the USPS post office.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swansea%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Swansea lies flat, stretching north–south along Illinois Route 159, a corridor that once supported early rail traffic.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swansea%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The village once housed underground coal mines, foundries, brickyards, and the Gundlach Drill Works, makers of efficient grain drills.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swansea%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Swansea is bordered by Fairview Heights, Belleville, O’Fallon, and Shiloh, forming a tight-knit cluster of southern Illinois communities.",
